BENCHMARK EXECUTION CONTRACT

Run identity / isolation
- Model: `ox-alpha`.
- All project files must live under `C:\Users\Epic\Documents\ChatGPT\Ox model test`.
- Required folder: `<Project Name> [model:ox-alpha] [<harness-key>] [run:<NN>]`.
- Use a short lowercase harness key. Start at `run:01`; if that project/model/harness already exists, atomically claim the next available two-digit run number.
- Never overwrite, reuse, rename over, or modify another run's folder. Keep project dependencies/artifacts inside this run folder where practical.

Browser-only + parallel testing
- Finished project must run in a standard modern browser. Local dev servers are fine; Electron/Tauri/native runtimes are not substitutes.
- Assume many runs test simultaneously. Use a unique free localhost port, isolated browser profile/context, and run-scoped temp/cache/download/trace/screenshot artifacts.
- Never hijack, navigate, close, or kill unrelated browser tabs/processes/servers, and never kill something merely to free a port.
- Track/clean up only resources created by this run. Prefer headless testing unless visible interaction is genuinely required.

- Keep critical runtime assets/dependencies local or bundled when practical so the finished project does not unnecessarily depend on fragile third-party CDNs/APIs just to boot.
- Handle browser user-gesture restrictions correctly for features such as audio, fullscreen, pointer lock, clipboard, autoplay, or similar browser-gated capabilities; provide a clear user action instead of silently failing.
- If the project has non-obvious controls or shortcuts, expose them clearly in the experience through a controls/help overlay, onboarding hint, pause/menu screen, or similarly discoverable UI.

Execution efficiency
- Prefer the shortest direct implementation path that satisfies the task; investigate only enough to unblock building.
- Batch related checks when practical. Use bounded retries and switch strategy when an approach repeatedly fails.
- Preserve working progress. Finish the core vertical slice before extras.
- Avoid scope creep, prolonged architecture archaeology, over-debugging non-blockers, and stalling on optional services/assets; use sensible local/generated/mock fallbacks when useful.
- Verify real functionality, but do not run huge regression/stress/profiling suites unless a discovered issue warrants them.

Quality + performance
- Build a polished, coherent finished experience, not a checklist demo.
- Prioritize usability, visual quality, interaction feel, responsive feedback, consistent styling, and sensible design decisions.
- Avoid unfinished/debug/placeholder-looking final states when better treatment materially improves the result.
- Implement important behavior for real rather than faking it for screenshots. Keep code organized without overengineering.
- Keep runtime smooth/resource-conscious: avoid unbounded objects/effects/listeners/workers/requests, unnecessary per-frame work, runaway CPU/GPU/memory use, oversized assets, and performance that degrades over time.
- Reuse/pool/batch/cull/pause/clean up work when useful. Optimize the actual experience, not synthetic numbers, and do not sacrifice meaningful quality just to chase metrics.

Optional capabilities
- You may use any additional input modalities, inspection capabilities, or environment-provided tools when genuinely useful. Their use is optional; do not create extra work simply because they exist.

OPTIONAL CAPABILITY — ISOLATED E2E TESTING (contested-machine browser protocol)
If this run does any browser automation / E2E testing / screenshots and
{{E2E_ISOLATION_SKILL_PATH}} is not UNAVAILABLE, read and follow that SKILL.md
before your first browser/playwright step. Core rules it enforces: prefer
deterministic non-browser tests; ONE tab maximum (a second client is a raw
in-page WebSocket or node script, never a second tab); stamp every tab
(window.__TID) and re-assert stamp+URL before EVERY browser tool call;
abandon silently any tab showing someone else's page; keep trigger→assert
steps atomic under UI fade/expiry windows; clean up only process PIDs you
recorded spawning — never name-filter sweeps. Skill artifacts stay inside
this run's project folder. If the path is UNAVAILABLE, proceed using the
isolation rules above.

IMAGE GENERATION — USE THIS SKILL, NOTHING ELSE
If image generation/editing would genuinely help, use only:
`C:\Users\Epic\.agents\skills\gpt-image-2\scripts\gpt_image.mjs`
No API keys or browser automation for image generation.

Rules:
- Node 18+; always pass `-o`; parse stdout exactly.
- Do not wrap/parallelize the script or create sleep-loop retry logic; keep `--delay-ms >= 1500`.
- Plus capacity is limited (~50 images/3h), so wait instead of retry-spamming limits.
- On 401, tell the user to run `codex login`.
- Sizes: `1024x1024|1024x1536|1536x1024`; quality: `low|medium|high`.

Single:
`node "C:\Users\Epic\.agents\skills\gpt-image-2\scripts\gpt_image.mjs" -o out.png "PROMPT"`
Successful stdout = exactly one line, the absolute PNG path.

Batch:
`node "C:\Users\Epic\.agents\skills\gpt-image-2\scripts\gpt_image.mjs" --n 10 --quality low -o sheet.png "PROMPT"`
Outputs `sheet-1.png`...`sheet-10.png`; exit `0` all ok / `2` partial / `1` fail; logs on stderr.

Edit:
`node "C:\Users\Epic\.agents\skills\gpt-image-2\scripts\gpt_image.mjs" --ref input.png -o out.png "INSTRUCTION"`

For >3 images/background work:
`node "C:\Users\Epic\.agents\skills\gpt-image-2\scripts\gpt_image.mjs" queue add [-o x.png] "PROMPT"`
`node "C:\Users\Epic\.agents\skills\gpt-image-2\scripts\gpt_image.mjs" queue run --detach`
`node "C:\Users\Epic\.agents\skills\gpt-image-2\scripts\gpt_image.mjs" queue status --json`
Poll about every 20s until `pending==0 && running==0`.
Use `queue list --json` for `.path`/`.error`; retry failed items with `queue run --retry-failed`.

CONDITIONAL MULTIPLAYER BUILD CONTRACT v2
Applies only if multiplayer is included. Epic handles deployment; you build only.
- Produce ONE self-contained static build folder suitable for `OxAlpha/<Project>/<Harness>/`.
- Do NOT deploy, push, SSH, edit compose files, touch Traefik, or run anything on the server.
- Multiplayer build folder: `<Project>-<Harness>` (Hermes harness unless told otherwise).
- Entry: root `index.html`, or `public/index.html` with that structure intact.
- `arcade.json` beside entry:
  `{"title":"<Human Name>","description":"<one line>","multiplayer":{"endpoint":"/ws/<project-slug>"}}`
- Slug must be lowercase `[a-z0-9-]` and match the `server.mjs` route exactly.

Optional backend:
- If needed, add ONE `server.mjs` beside entry: ESM, Node builtins only, no npm packages, no `listen()`, no spawned server.
- Platform loads a default export with optional `maxSockets`, optional `tickMs`, and `create(ctx)` returning `open`, `message`, `close`, optional `tick`, optional `stop`.
- `ws` provides `{ id, send(obj|string), close(code), query(URLSearchParams), ip }`.
- State is memory-only; clients reconnect/rejoin after restart. Keep handlers deterministic/fast. If offline-only, omit `server.mjs`.

Client netcode:
- Same-origin/subpath-safe only:
  `const url=(location.protocol==="https:"?"wss://":"ws://")+location.host+"/ws/<project-slug>";`
- Never hardcode domains/ports/`ws://localhost` or root-absolute asset/worker/importScripts paths.
- Auto-reconnect with backoff and graceful disconnected UI.
- Netcode belongs in normal source dirs.
- Game must still boot pure-static if backend is down, and from `file://` or a dumb static server.

Multiplayer handoff: state Project, Harness, entry path, endpoint, and message protocol/event payloads.

FINAL VERIFICATION / HANDOFF
- Launch the real project in a browser and exercise the core experience enough to catch obvious blockers.
- Screenshots are mandatory. Capture real running states inside this run's project folder, inspect them yourself, and fix obvious broken/blank/loading/unusable visuals.
- Screenshots must show the actual result, not merely an editor, terminal, loading screen, or blank browser.
- Fix obvious stutter, major loading/resource issues, degraded-over-time performance, broken interactions, and major usability problems encountered during normal use.
- Do not claim completion if the core experience is broken.

Create concise `RESULT.md` with:
Project; Model `ox-alpha`; Harness; Run; Status (completed/partial/failed); launch instructions; screenshot paths; major known issues; duration/turn count if exposed.

A normal README + `RESULT.md` is sufficient unless the project genuinely needs more documentation.

Create an ORIGINAL, polished, complete browser-based GACHA RPG from scratch.

You are responsible for the ENTIRE project:

CONCEPT
→ WORLD
→ STORY
→ CHARACTERS
→ ART DIRECTION
→ GAMEPLAY
→ EXPLORATION
→ PROGRESSION
→ GACHA SYSTEM
→ CHARACTER ABILITIES
→ UI
→ AUDIO
→ ASSETS
→ IMPLEMENTATION
→ TESTING
→ BALANCING
→ VISUAL QA
→ POLISH.

I am intentionally NOT giving you the game's setting, characters, story, title, or primary gameplay mechanic.

Invent them yourself.

SURPRISE ME.

The result should feel like the beginning of a REAL original gacha game/IP rather than a tech demo with a random summon button.

==================================================
IMPORTANT — NO REAL-MONEY GAMBLING
==================================================

This is a gameplay/technical experiment.

DO NOT implement:

- real-money purchases;
- payment processing;
- purchasable premium currency;
- cryptocurrency;
- cash-out systems;
- gambling involving anything of real-world value.

All summon currency must be earned/generated entirely inside the local game.

The gacha exists as a progression/collection mechanic for this prototype.

==================================================
PROJECT LOCATION + RUN ISOLATION
==================================================

Root directory:

C:\Users\Epic\Documents\ChatGPT\Ox model test

Invent an original filesystem-safe title for the game.

Create a NEW isolated project directory using:

`<Project Name> [model:ox-alpha] [<harness-key>] [run:<NN>]`

Use a concise execution-environment/session identifier if one is automatically and reliably available. Otherwise generate a short unique run identifier.

Do not modify another run's project. Keep source, assets, generated artwork, models, audio, screenshots, tests, configuration, and documentation isolated inside this project wherever practical.

==================================================
FIRST ACTION — INSPECT YOUR SKILLS
==================================================

BEFORE designing the game:

Inspect ALL relevant skills, tools and specialized capabilities available in your current environment.

Specifically investigate capabilities involving:

- GPT Image 2;
- image generation;
- image editing;
- concept art;
- textures;
- 3D;
- Blender;
- modeling;
- animation;
- Fish Audio;
- audio generation;
- voice;
- music;
- sound effects;
- browser game development;
- UI/UX;
- browser automation;
- screenshot analysis;
- testing;
- performance profiling.

READ the instructions for useful skills before using them.

Use relevant skills where they materially improve the project.

Do not use every skill merely because it exists.

==================================================
GPT IMAGE 2 — MANDATORY
==================================================

GPT Image 2 is a REQUIRED major part of this project's creative pipeline.

DO NOT satisfy this requirement by generating:

one background

or

one title image.

Use GPT Image 2 extensively and intelligently to establish the game's VISUAL IDENTITY.

Potential uses include:

- character concept sheets;
- character splash art;
- character portraits;
- expressions;
- environments;
- locations;
- factions;
- enemies;
- creatures;
- items;
- weapons/tools;
- artifacts;
- UI artwork;
- icons;
- banners;
- story illustrations;
- loading screens;
- promotional/key artwork;
- textures;
- decals;
- environmental graphics.

Choose applications appropriate to the game you invent.

==================================================
CHARACTER DESIGN — MAJOR GPT IMAGE 2 TEST
==================================================

Create an ORIGINAL launch roster.

Aim for approximately:

8-12 meaningful playable characters

if feasible within the goal.

QUALITY AND CONSISTENCY ARE MORE IMPORTANT THAN RAW CHARACTER COUNT.

Every major character should have a deliberate identity.

Define things such as:

- name;
- personality;
- silhouette;
- visual motif;
- faction/origin;
- role;
- gameplay identity;
- abilities;
- color language;
- important accessories;
- character relationships;
- rarity where applicable.

Use GPT Image 2 during character development.

==================================================
CHARACTER CONSISTENCY — CRITICAL
==================================================

THIS IS ONE OF THE PRIMARY TESTS.

A character must remain recognizably THE SAME CHARACTER across different generated assets.

For example:

CONCEPT
→ SPLASH ART
→ PORTRAIT
→ STORY IMAGE
→ CHARACTER MENU
→ OTHER REPRESENTATIONS

should preserve important defining characteristics.

Do NOT repeatedly generate characters from vague descriptions and accept major design drift.

Establish a CHARACTER BIBLE for each important character.

Record defining visual details.

Where the available image-generation workflow supports image references/editing/iteration, use them intelligently to preserve consistency.

Inspect generated artwork.

Reject/regenerate/edit outputs where a character loses important identity.

==================================================
VISUAL BIBLE
==================================================

Create an internal visual bible establishing:

- overall art direction;
- world architecture;
- environments;
- character design language;
- factions;
- color language;
- lighting;
- materials;
- UI language;
- iconography;
- recurring symbols;
- technology/magic rules where applicable.

GPT Image 2 should help DEVELOP this visual bible.

The final game should look like ONE coherent IP.

==================================================
DO NOT JUST GENERATE ART — TRANSLATE IT
==================================================

This is critical.

GPT Image 2 concept art should influence the ACTUAL GAME.

Use the concepts as references when creating:

- environments;
- geometry;
- models;
- materials;
- textures;
- lighting;
- UI;
- effects;
- character representations.

Periodically:

CAPTURE ACTUAL GAME SCREENSHOT
→ INSPECT IT
→ COMPARE AGAINST VISUAL TARGET
→ IDENTIFY GAP
→ IMPROVE GAME
→ CAPTURE AGAIN.

Do not produce gorgeous concept art followed by a game consisting of primitive gray cubes.

==================================================
GAME TYPE
==================================================

Build a genuine playable RPG/adventure experience.

Choose the exact format yourself.

Potential directions include:

- 3D exploration;
- stylized open zones;
- mission-based areas;
- puzzle exploration;
- action RPG;
- tactical RPG;
- hybrid real-time gameplay;
- another original approach.

Do NOT automatically create generic turn-based combat simply because "gacha game" was specified.

Choose gameplay that best serves YOUR concept.

==================================================
WORLD
==================================================

Create a coherent original world.

The player should have somewhere interesting to EXIST beyond menus.

Build meaningful environments.

Include where feasible:

- hub area;
- explorable spaces;
- NPCs;
- interactable objects;
- environmental storytelling;
- quests/objectives;
- secrets;
- progression.

Do not make the entire game:

MENU
→ BATTLE
→ MENU
→ GACHA.

==================================================
CORE GAMEPLAY LOOP
==================================================

Create a satisfying loop conceptually similar to:

EXPLORE
→ COMPLETE OBJECTIVES
→ EARN RESOURCES
→ DEVELOP CHARACTERS
→ UNLOCK CONTENT
→ SUMMON
→ BUILD ROSTER
→ TACKLE NEW CHALLENGES
→ DISCOVER STORY.

Adapt this to your own design.

==================================================
GACHA SYSTEM
==================================================

Implement a REAL functional summon system.

Design:

- rarity tiers;
- rates;
- banners;
- single summon;
- multi-summon;
- pity/guarantee mechanics;
- duplicate handling;
- summon history;
- currency earning;
- inventory/roster integration.

Display rates clearly.

Use deterministic/testable RNG architecture where appropriate.

==================================================
NO DARK-PATTERN GARBAGE
==================================================

Although this is a gacha-game experiment, do NOT intentionally implement predatory UX.

Avoid:

- fake countdown pressure;
- deceptive odds;
- fake "almost won" animations;
- hidden probabilities;
- manipulative purchasing prompts.

There is no real money here anyway.

Focus on the FUN parts:

collecting characters, anticipation, progression and team building.

==================================================
SUMMON PRESENTATION
==================================================

MAKE SUMMONING FEEL GOOD.

Create a polished summon sequence.

Use:

- animation;
- visual effects;
- sound;
- rarity reveals;
- pacing;
- character reveals;
- skip functionality.

Higher-rarity pulls should feel meaningfully different.

GPT Image 2 character artwork should shine here.

Do not make summons appear as:

alert("You got Bob")

💀

==================================================
CHARACTER COLLECTION
==================================================

Create a polished roster/collection interface.

Allow:

- browsing owned characters;
- inspecting characters;
- viewing artwork;
- viewing rarity;
- viewing stats;
- viewing abilities;
- sorting/filtering;
- selecting teams where relevant.

Locked/unowned characters may be represented appropriately without confusing them with owned units.

==================================================
CHARACTER PROGRESSION
==================================================

Implement meaningful progression.

Potential systems:

- levels;
- experience;
- abilities;
- upgrades;
- equipment;
- skill trees;
- ascension;
- relationship systems.

Choose a coherent subset.

Do not implement fifteen shallow progression currencies.

==================================================
CHARACTER GAMEPLAY IDENTITY
==================================================

Characters should NOT merely have different portraits and numbers.

Different characters should meaningfully affect gameplay.

Give them distinct:

- abilities;
- movement;
- mechanics;
- roles;
- synergies;
- strengths/weaknesses

where appropriate to your gameplay system.

==================================================
STORY
==================================================

Create an original narrative foundation.

Introduce:

- world;
- protagonist/player role;
- central mystery/conflict;
- important characters;
- factions;
- reasons to explore;
- reasons characters join the player's roster.

Do not dump fourteen paragraphs of exposition immediately.

Use environmental storytelling and character interactions where appropriate.

==================================================
AUDIO
==================================================

Audio is important.

After inspecting available skills, use appropriate capabilities for:

- music;
- ambience;
- UI sounds;
- gameplay SFX;
- summon sounds;
- character sounds;
- environmental audio.

If Fish Audio or another relevant audio/voice skill is available:

READ ITS SKILL INSTRUCTIONS.

Use it where it genuinely improves the experience.

If character voices are feasible, give important characters appropriate ORIGINAL performances.

Do not imitate living actors or copyrighted character voices.

==================================================
3D / BLENDER
==================================================

If the game uses 3D and relevant Blender/3D skills are available:

USE THEM where they improve quality.

Conceptually:

GPT IMAGE 2 CONCEPT
→ MODELING REFERENCE
→ 3D ASSET
→ MATERIAL/TEXTURE
→ GAME
→ SCREENSHOT
→ VISUAL COMPARISON
→ ITERATE.

Do not blindly produce assets without inspecting them.

==================================================
UI
==================================================

Create a premium game UI.

Important interfaces may include:

- title screen;
- main menu;
- HUD;
- character roster;
- character details;
- team management;
- summon banner;
- summon results;
- inventory;
- quests/objectives;
- settings.

Do NOT make it look like a generic SaaS dashboard.

It should look like a GAME.

==================================================
CONTROLS — CRITICAL
==================================================

Controls must NOT be accidentally inverted.

If 3D:

W = forward
S = backward
A = left
D = right

Mouse RIGHT = look right.
Mouse LEFT = look left.
Mouse UP = look up.
Mouse DOWN = look down.

If another control scheme is used, follow normal conventions.

TEST resulting movement/camera state.

Do not merely test that input events fired.

==================================================
LOCALHOST / PORT ISOLATION
==================================================

Many autonomous projects may currently be running.

Before starting a server:

1. inspect listening ports;
2. choose an AVAILABLE port;
3. record it;
4. use it consistently.

If a port is occupied:

LEAVE IT ALONE.

Do NOT kill another project's server.

Do NOT broadly kill Node, Python, browsers, Docker, agents or development servers.

Only terminate processes positively identified as belonging to THIS game.

DO NOT START THE GREAT LOCALHOST WAR OF 2026.

==================================================
HEADLESS DEVELOPMENT / TESTING
==================================================

Automated interaction MUST be headless.

Do NOT:

- control my browser;
- move my cursor;
- steal focus;
- type on my desktop;
- close unrelated applications.

Use isolated headless browser automation.

==================================================
FINAL GAMEPLAY VERIFICATION
==================================================

Before handoff, actually play a representative slice and confirm movement/exploration, character abilities, progression/currency earning, summoning, roster/team use, and save/reload work together. Fix blocking or obviously broken behavior.

For the gacha logic, run targeted scripted sanity checks for rates/configuration, currency deductions, pity/guarantees, and duplicate handling. Large Monte Carlo simulations and statistical reports are optional unless needed to diagnose a problem.

SAVE SYSTEM
==================================================

Persist:

- progression;
- roster;
- currency;
- pity;
- summon history;
- completed objectives;
- settings

where appropriate.

Test save/reload.

==================================================
VISUAL QA — REQUIRED
==================================================

Screenshots are mandatory. Capture at least 2 real in-game screenshots from representative states, including normal 3D gameplay and a key character/gacha/UI moment. Inspect the actual pixels, compare them to the intended art direction, and fix obvious visual problems before handoff. Additional screenshot iterations are welcome when useful but are not required as an exhaustive matrix.

PERFORMANCE
==================================================

Profile the actual game.

Avoid destroying visual quality merely because headless software rendering is slower.

Create QA graphics settings if necessary.

The real player-facing experience may target the machine's discrete GPU.

==================================================
SUBAGENTS — OX ALPHA ONLY
==================================================

You may delegate work when useful.

EVERY SUBAGENT MUST USE:

OX ALPHA.

STRICTLY.

Do NOT delegate to:

- Auto routing;
- another provider/model.

If you cannot guarantee Ox Alpha:

DO NOT DELEGATE.

Perform the work yourself.

No wallet jumpscares.

==================================================
ITERATIVE DEVELOPMENT
==================================================

Keep the build process practical:

1. Concept + visual direction
2. Core 3D gameplay foundation
3. Characters/world/content
4. Gacha/progression/UI/audio
5. Performance + final verification/polish

Combine or reorder work as useful. Do not create separate mandatory statistical QA, regression, or screenshot passes unless the project actually needs them.

DEFINITION OF DONE
==================================================

The final result should make me think:

"Wait... Ox made an actual gacha game?"

NOT:

"Ox made a gacha menu."

It needs:

- original IP;
- coherent world;
- original roster;
- consistent characters;
- GPT Image 2-driven visual direction;
- actual gameplay;
- exploration/content;
- meaningful character differences;
- functional gacha;
- progression;
- polished summoning;
- audio;
- persistence;
- strong UI;
- visual consistency;
- automated gameplay verification.

==================================================
FINAL REPORT
==================================================

Report:

- game title;
- runner/environment;
- project directory;
- localhost URL;
- concept;
- world;
- launch roster;
- GPT Image 2 assets generated;
- how GPT Image 2 influenced actual game assets;
- character-consistency process;
- other skills used;
- gameplay systems;
- gacha rates/pity;
- statistical gacha verification;
- progression;
- audio;
- save system;
- visual QA;
- gameplay QA;
- full-playthrough result;
- performance;
- subagents used;
- confirmation all subagents used Ox Alpha;
- remaining limitations.

Do not show me what you COULD build.

BUILD IT.

MAKE AN IP.

MAKE ME WANT THE CHARACTERS.

MAKE THE PULL ANIMATION HIT.

AND MAKE GPT IMAGE 2 COOK. 🔥

